On 2008-03-14, ghost@256k.org <ghost@256k.org> wrote:
> Still, i think that polypiling as it stands now is a little broken,
> partly because it's too powerful, but moreso because it encourages
> tedious play. Polypiling is so useful that if I don't use a wand to
> its fullest (carefully arranging items into stacks with even
> distribution of materials), I almost feel like I'm playing a conduct
> game.

I understand the feeling.  I have always been a reluctant polypiler, if
only because it increases the # of conducts at the end -- though really,
polyobject is one of the easiest to keep.  (and ironically, polyself is
one of the hardest unless you play a vanilla Wizard.)

> I think one solution might be to make the wand/spell of polymorph not
> affect more than one square. 

It's hard to arrange for the player to be swarmed by golems in this
case.  Polypiling is a high-reward activity; ideally, it should carry
high risk as well, and then we don't _need_ to remove the reward.

> One other thought on increasing the risk of poly zaps: every zap could
> have a small chance of generating an air or earth elemental ("The very
> air changes character and draws itself together"), or a water or fire
> if water/lava squares are nearby. Elementals are generally scarier
> than golems, especially since people polypile iron and stone things
> less often than scrolls and spellbooks. Also, it would be neat if
> golems generated from a pile of exclusively blessed objects had a
> chance of generating tame, but then that kind of takes away from the
> risk factor...

Perhaps not elementals (air elementals would be unduly harsh, fire
elementals crunchy), but i'm definitely still leaning towards the idea
of increasing golem risk (ie. create a circumstance where there is -no-
safe way to polymorph more than one object per square) and creating
newer, more obnoxious golems that only show up from polypiling to add 
to the fun.

